        "successFeedback": "A(3) = 500 · 2³ = 4000."
      },
      "variant": {
        "gen": "exp-function",
        "form": "growthModel"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "The starting amount is the value at x = 0. For P(x) = 200 · 3ˣ, P(0) = 200 — the population before any growth. Each year then multiplies by 3: 200, 600, 1800, and so on.",
      "figure": "exp-p0-200"
    },
    {
      "id": "i2",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Read the start.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "For P(x) = 200 · 3^x, what is the starting value P(0)?",
        "answer": 200,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 600,
            "feedback": "That's P(1), after one year. At x = 0, 3⁰ = 1, so P(0) = 200."
          },
          {
            "value": 3,
            "feedback": "3 is the growth factor, not the start. P(0) = 200."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "P(0) = 200 · 3⁰ = 200."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c3",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "Bigger bases grow faster. Compare doubling (×2) with quadrupling (×4): after the same number of steps, the ×4 model is far larger. The base is the engine of exponential growth.",
